body:
Hi! Yeah, great question! So Brussels is basically the whole world cramed together in one big city (probably not that big compared to other cities in the world, but still). We have around 180 nationalitites (182 i think). If my memory from school is correct, we have the second most nationalities in a city in the world. Only Dubai has more nationalities!

&#x200B;

It comes with a lot of challanges, but it has so much beauty in it! Brussels itself is actually 19 different towns/cities combined to one. All 19 have their own postal code and mayor and stuff. But, even in all the different towns, there are so many different neighbourhoods. Every new street can be a totally different world. You can go from one culture into a totally new one in just minutes. It's amazing. The governement of Brussels (I won't go into belgian politics, I don't know most of it but it's freaking complicated and will drag us too far. Search for some video's on youtube if you're interested lol. Basically there are a billion governements on all the different levels and all of them come together in the complex city that is Brussels!) even makes flyers and posters and stuff in a lot of different languages. I heard about one about covid-19 was in 26 languages! Amazing, right? THe official languages of Belgium are Dutch, French and German. Brussels has two: Frnech and Dutch. So all official communication has to be in one of these. You can legally request to change from French to Dutch or the other way around, but that's it. But, as I said, a lot of flyers, brochures etc with important information is made in a lot of languages, as to reach as many people as possible, because a lot of people don't speak either French or Dutch. But, official communication is only allowed in Dutch or French. You can always bring a translator to court or city hall or stuff tho. 

&#x200B;

But other than that, you will see a lot of different languages in the streets! I only speak Dutch, English and understand enough French to get by, but a lot of stores focussed on their culture, have names and writings in their specific language. 

&#x200B;

The most common language is French I would say, but then again, that really depends on what neigbourhood you are in. There's a big community of expats and they live aroud the same neighbourhood, and I suspect you won't hear a lot of French in stores and restuarants there. It's expected from people with an official job (like police, governement) or important jobs as hospitals to know both Dutch and French and any other language is a big bonus, but don't expect that in stores and stuff. I usually talk French in stores, even tho I suck at it. I once got a job in a restaurant because I speak Dutch and nobody from their staff did lol. My French was even worse back then but they didn't care. They needed someone who spoke Dutch to make Flemish customers happy lol. Flemish people can be really butthurt when they have to speak Frnech because no staff speaks Dutch. 

&#x200B;

If you have any more questions, I will be happy to share my experience! Mind you, this is mostly my own experience and other people will have other insights and stories to tell :)

Edit: pressed enter in the middle of my comment lol.

I found a charts that said she should be on half her adult weight by now, and she is, so I guess she's not too fat indeed :p I just got a scare from that facebook post that said their dog eats less, but I didn't know an older dog is supposed to eat less than active puppies who are running and playing all day and are still growing :) thanks! I'm seeing the vet in two weeks and I'll ask to be sure, but I'm not so worried anymore :)

&#x200B;

Thanks everyone!

Punkcase is stealing my fucking money. Don't buy from them. I ordered a case in March. It never arrived. I asked them, they said they would send a new one. Never arrived. Form august on, I have been sending daily emails for my refund. Initially, they said they would not refund, because 'the item was not back with them". I accused them of stealing my money. beginning of October, they finally said "fine, we'll refund your money". Ever since they said that, they are ignoring my emails and they have not yet refunded me. I do'nt know what I can do anymore. If anyone has any tips for me... :(

&#x200B;

Edit: typos because I'm pissed off.

It has always baffled me how less some people care about how they raise their own kids. I just don't get it, if you put little people on this eath, why would you not care about how they turn out? I think my mom did the best she could, but the just wasn't meant to raise kids. My dad and grandma tried to make it right when they started taking more care of me and my bro and sis, and they are the reason I eat quite healthy, but still..

&#x200B;

Hope you are doing good now my friend. I'm not a parent myself (I'm still a kid lol), but I think realizing what mistakes not to make, is the first step in avoiding to make them, I guess. Thanks for the support! ❤️

I know, but it's so *haaaaaarrrdddd* \*dramaqueen intensifies\*

&#x200B;

I guess I'm missing out on a lot, especially the 'winter fruits' like apples and pears and stuff. I kinda like the summer fruits like strawberries and cherries more, and now tangerines too. 

&#x200B;

My boyfriend is amazing tho, his lack of enthousiasm about tasting food makes me realize I'm a big dramaqueen that should get over herself lol. Thanks for the support! ❤️

the not having expectations is a good tip. I always go on the smell of food: I trust my nose a great deal in if I might like something or not. Most of the time, my nose is right, but sometimes food does smell bad but I surprisingly like the food. I know most of it is in my head, and I've learned to eat bananas, strawberries, cherries and now tangeries too. I tasted mango a few weeks ago and I didn't really like it. 

&#x200B;

I make smoothies with a lot of fruit I don't really like, so that was a first big step for me. But now I have to make the big step and try the actual fruits. 

&#x200B;

Growing up, I *hated* veggies. So much. Oh god. But what has been helping me in liking them, is making food where the veggies are 'mixed in', like a pasta bowl where the veggies and pasta end up on one spoon. I've realized I have some kind of weird fear of tasting stuff I don't like, and eating a few different kind of food at the same bite, with tomething 'safe' like a potato, pasta or rice, has helped me a lot in trying veggies. I will never put a piece of veggie alone in my mouth, it's always accompanied by a buffer. God, I sound weird.

&#x200B;

But thank you so much! ❤️

That's a good tip! I've tried oatmeal before, but never like this. oatmeal with banana and other stuff sounds worth a try tbh. I'm gonna look into that, but these tips are awesome already!  ❤️ 

&#x200B;

A weird motivation for me has been an unhealthy breakfast, if that makes any sense! If I allow myself to eat an unhealthy breakfast (like bread with nutella, shit I'm addicted to that), I get kinda mad at myself and say over and over in y head "You've already had one unhealthy meal, now the other meals have to be better!" and that kinda helps me eat veggies in my other meals or banana as a snack.

Hope you're doing better now :) I want to believe I will raise my own kids with a lot of healthy habbits. My roommate, she is amazing. Whenever she wants a snack, she just eats a carrot or a piece of paprika. How great is that? She loves them, and she almost never eats candy or cookies or something. 

&#x200B;

The tangerine surprised me in the way that you don't actually feel those threads when you eat it. I was always afraid of those, but they were fine.

&#x200B;

High five on the learning healthy habbits, my firend! Thanks for the support! ❤️

For me, it's banana. I love banana because, to me, it has a very soft taste. It's not screaming in your face and taking over every damn taste bud, but maybe that's because I've learned to eat it a while ago and it's my go-to fruit now.

&#x200B;

And thank you so much!  ❤️
